tail - the last lines

SYNOPSIS
    tail [-n COUNT] [-f] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last ten lines, or COUNT of them.

      -n COUNT   how many
      -f         keep printing whatever is appended, until Ctrl-C

    A file cannot be polled - it is always ready, because a read returns
    at once whether or not anything was added - so -f watches the size and
    sleeps between looks, which is what it is underneath on any system
    without change notification. A file that has become shorter has been
    rewritten rather than appended to, so following starts again from the
    beginning: a log that has just been rotated looks exactly like that.

    One file at a time. Following several means interleaving them and
    reprinting a header whenever the source changes, which is a different
    program.

SEE ALSO
    head, less, cat
